        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "### N170 Component Characteristics\n",
        "The polarity of the N170 ERP is negative, displaying a voltage peak at a latency of approximately 170ms post-stimulus onset (@Gao_et_al.2019). The most topographically active areas when the N170 is elicited are the bilateral occipito-temporal areas, specifically, electrodes P7, P8, PO7 and PO8 from a standard 10-20 system (@Johnston_et_al.2015;@Navajas_et_al.2013)\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background Research on the N170 Component\n",
        "#### Initial Observations of the N170\n",
        "The N170 was first officially observed by @Bentin_et_al.1996, utilising electroencephalography (EEG) to study facial perception. @Bentin_et_al.1996 found that visual stimuli of an upright human face elicited an N170 at approximately 172ms, with prominence over the posterior temporal scalp, particularly over the right hemisphere. The prominence over the right hemisphere may now be understood through the fusiform face area, which is often right-lateralized and is believed to undertake a crucial role in gestalt facial processing (@Kanwisher_et_al.1997). @Bentin_et_al.1996 found that distorted human faces evoked the same amplitude and latency as normal human faces, while inverted human faces displayed a similar amplitude to upright human faces, albeit, with a slightly delayed N170 component. However, @Bentin_et_al.1996 found that the N170 was not elicited by animal faces, human hands or inanimate objects, positing that the N170 may reflect the brain’s recognition of human faces. @Bentin_et_al.1996 further posit the N170's reflection of human facial features, particularly of the eyes,as sole depiction of eyes produced a greater amplitude for the N170 than that of a whole, intact, upright human face (@Bentin_et_al.1996). While human facial features such as the nose and mouth still elicited an N170, the amplitude of such features was less than that of an intact, upright human face (@Bentin_et_al.1996).\n",
        "\n",
        "Despite the first formal publication of the N170 from @Bentin_et_al.1996, @Bötzel_&_Grüsser.1989 were the first to informally observe  findings related to facial perception through the component now known as the Vertex Positive Potential (VPP). @Bötzel_&_Grüsser.1989 observed the VPP after showing participants visual stimuli of human faces amongst visual controls of chairs and trees. The VPP occurred at electrode Cz between 150ms to 200ms post-stimulus onset of the human faces. Following this observation, @Bötzel_et_al.1995 utilised a larger number of electrodes and found that the VPP was a direct three-dimensional correspondent to the N170, found at the occipito-temporal sites. Finally, @Bötzel_et_al.1995 undertook advanced computer analysis of dipoles, finding that the N170 electrical signals originated deep in the temporal cortex, crossing the visual cortex and centre of the scalp. Prior to @Bötzel_&_Grüsser.1989 use of EEG to understand human facial processing, researchers largely relied on invasive animal studies and research with prosopagnosia patients (@Taubert_et_al.2020; @Albonico_&_Barton.2019). Thus, the works of @Bötzel_&_Grüsser.1989 serve as a pivotal methodological advance through improving non-invasive techniques and the populations studies can be undertaken with to increase understanding of human facial processing.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Conditions where the N170 Component is elicited\n",
        "The classic visual stimuli utilised to elicit the N170 component is human facial stimuli (@Eimer.2011). Inverted human faces similarly elicit the N170 component, however, with an increased amplitude and delayed deflection comparatively (@Civile_et_al.2018). Varying human facial expression, such as fear, have been seen to inflate the N170  amplitude (@Blau_et_al.2007). Human eyes are a salient visual stimuli of the N170, eliciting a greater amplitude than that of a whole human face (@Bentin_et_al.1996). However, visual stimuli of the nose and lips display a delay in the N170 and decrease in amplitude compared to visual stimuli of a whole human face (@Bentin_et_al.1996). While the N170 ERP component is particularly sensitive to facial visual stimuli, it can also be elicited by written words (@Eimer.2011; @Maurer_et_al.2005). \n",
        "\n",
        "#### Functional Significance of the N170 Component\n",
        " The N170 is thought to represent visual facial detection and encoding in a gestalt manner (@Olivares_et_al.2015). \n",
        "\n",
        "Furthermore, the N170 can be applied clinically as a quantitative biomarker for autistic spectrum disorder, schizophrenia and developmental prosopagnosia, as such conditions display an uncharacteristic N170 component (@Johnston_et_al.2015).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Controversies regarding the N170\n",
        "Due to the relatively new use of EEG for facial perception studies  (@Bentin_et_al.1996), there is some controvery over what the N170 truly reflects. A topic that is strongly debated at current is whether the N170 reflects face-specificty. @Rossion_et_al.2000 propose that there may be face-specific processes in areas of the brain, such as the fusiform gyrus, while a greater number of works posit that such areas may better reflect stimuli processing from an expert of perception within this field (@Carmel_&_Bentin.2002; @Dering_et_al.2012). However, @Ganis_et_al.2012 promote a sensible view of the reflections of the N170: this ERP component responds favourably to face and face-like stimuli compared to other categories of stimuli such as objects, and scrambled pictures. \n",
        "\n",
        "@Sagehorn_et_al..2023 presents another interesting debate regarding the N170, finding that the N170 lost discrimination power of faces when participants viewed visual stimuli through virtual reality (VR), as opposed to the traditional 2D display of the visual stimuli. Such conclusions present the possibility that results of the N170 from 2D visual representations may not applicable to VR visual stimuli, and further, applications to processing of real-life faces.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Methods\n",
        "\n",
        "### Participants\n",
        "Initially, a total of 40 participants were recruited for the current study by @Kappenman_et_al.2021. This sample contained 25 females and 15 males, with an average age of 21.5 years and a standard deviation of 2.87 years. The range of ages was 18 to 30 and 38 participants were right handed. All participants are neurotypical. However, results from three participants were excluded due to artifact rejection. Therefore, results were analysed from 37 participants. The new sample contained 24 females and 13 males, with an average age of 21.5 years and a standard deviation of 2.96 years. The age range of participants was 18 to 30 years and 35 participants were right-handed. Inclusion criteria included native English speaking competence, normal colour perception, normal or corrected-to-normal vision and no history of a neurological disease or injury. Each participant provided a self-report regarding the inclusion criteria."
