        "## Discussion and reflection\n",
        "\n",
        "The expected experimental effects for the N170 grand average are observed. Each condition displays a P100 situated approximately around the 100ms interval, reflecting initial visual processing of each stimuli category (@Herzberg_et_al.2025). The N170 is observed with a shorter latency and increased amplitude for the facial condition compared to the car condition. The scrambled face and scrambled car stimuli depict similar amplitudes at approx 170ms, and do not reach closely to the amplitude of the unscrambled visual conditions. This could be due to the structural integrity of the concepts being destroyed through phase-scrambling, wherein, face-sensitive neurons do not fire at the same time, decreasing the observed amplitude (@Liu_et_al.2016). \n",
        "\n",
        "The N170 component within the current study was similar to the grand average observed in @Kappenman_et_al.2021. Both N170 components for the facial condition occur at approximately 170ms post-stimulus onset. However, the grand average from @Kappenman_et_al.2021 omits the scrambled face and scrambled car conditions, whereas, the  current grand average does not. We have included the scrambled conditions to showcase the difference between clear and scrambled images in the evoked response. Furthermore, @Kappenman_et_al.2021 plot negativity downwards on the y-axis, as opposed to the current grand average that plots negativity upwards on the y-axis, contributing to inverted wave forms. Furthermore, the y-axis for Kappenman et al's grand average ranges from -10uV to +15uV, while the y-axis of the current grand average ranges from -10uV to +10uV. The  larger range for the y-axis in @Kappenman_et_al.2021, the grand average smooths out sudden deflections, whereas, the current study's grand average displays smaller deflections in the waveform.  \n",
        "\n",
        "By applying the analysis process to the N170 dataset, I feel that I was given a chance to try to understand and integrate the information specific to the N170 dataset on my own. Making mistakes ultimately ended up being the best process for my learning, as I may make a mistake, but getting to go through the process of where I went wrong with Ina really helped me to understand why different parameters and processes practically matter in running the code. I feel that I understood the pipeline process to a greater degree by undertaking a new dataset, as I could flow through each step to understand why certain steps were undertaken before others, and importance of each step to the overall outcome of producing the desired results.\n",
